.TH "PROCMAIL\-RS.RC" "5" "" "File Formats Manual" "procmail\-rs"
.SH NAME
procmail\-rs.rc \- configuration language for procmail\-rs
.SH DESCRIPTION
An rc file is a UTF\-8 sequence of assignments and recipes evaluated in
source order.
It selects how \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R](1) examines and delivers one
byte\-oriented mail message.
The accepted language is intentionally smaller than procmail 3.22;
unsupported recognized forms are rejected instead of being approximated.
.PP
The root file is completely parsed and validated before message input is
read.
An \f[CR]INCLUDERC\f[R] or \f[CR]SWITCHRC\f[R] whose path depends on
message processing is loaded when execution reaches it and is subjected
to the same aggregate limits.
.SH LEXICAL RULES
Blank lines and lines whose first non\-whitespace byte is \f[CR]#\f[R]
are ignored.
Assignments have the form \f[CR]NAME=value\f[R].
A recipe begins with \f[CR]:0\f[R], contains zero or more condition
lines beginning with \f[CR]*\f[R], and ends in one action.
.PP
A standalone comment may occur between recipe conditions.
An assignment value is one shell\-like word.
After that word, optional whitespace followed by \f[CR]#\f[R] begins a
comment.
A \f[CR]#\f[R] within the word, inside either quote mode, after a
backslash, or inside a backquoted command is data.
A second unquoted word is rejected instead of being ignored.
A recipe header may also have a trailing comment.
Do not append an rc comment to a condition or destination action: there
it belongs to the regex, path, or shell text being parsed.
.PP
Pipe commands and recipe conditions may span physical lines.
Every continued pipe\-command line retains its trailing backslash for
the real shell.
A condition\(cqs trailing backslash and physical newline are removed;
leading space and tab on the following line are removed for ordinary
conditions and retained for shell\-expanded \f[CR]$\f[R] conditions.
Each physical line and the complete joined command or condition are
bounded by \f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R].
Unquoted, single\-quoted, and double\-quoted assignment fragments may be
concatenated without whitespace, as in
\f[CR]NAME=pre\-\(aqliteral\-\(aq\(dq$VALUE\(dq\f[R].
.PP
Physical rc lines, expanded values, and continued conditions and pipe
commands are bounded by the active \f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R].
The root rc file itself is bounded independently.
.SH VARIABLE EXPANSION
The supported forms are \f[CR]$NAME\f[R], \f[CR]${NAME}\f[R],
\f[CR]${NAME\-word}\f[R], \f[CR]${NAME:\-word}\f[R],
\f[CR]${NAME+word}\f[R], \f[CR]${NAME:+word}\f[R],
\f[CR]${NAME:=word}\f[R], and \f[CR]${NAME:?word}\f[R].
A name begins with an ASCII letter or underscore and continues with
ASCII letters, digits, or underscores.
The \f[CR]\-\f[R] form uses \f[CR]word\f[R] when the name is unset;
\f[CR]:\-\f[R] also treats an empty value as unset.
The \f[CR]+\f[R] form uses \f[CR]word\f[R] when the name is set;
\f[CR]:+\f[R] additionally requires a non\-empty value.
An unselected word is not evaluated, and \f[CR]word\f[R] may be empty.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(17.5n) lw(17.5n) lw(17.5n) lw(17.5n).
T{
Form
T}@T{
