T}
.TE
.PP
\f[CR]${NAME:=word}\f[R] is a procmail\-rs extension accepted in
assignment values and destination expressions.
\f[CR]NAME\f[R] must be an ordinary user variable; settings,
runtime\-produced names, and read\-only names cannot be changed this
way.
Changes are held until the complete expression succeeds, but later parts
of that same expression see them.
An error in any later part discards all such changes.
Other expression contexts reject \f[CR]:=\f[R] while preparing the rc
file.
.PP
\f[CR]${NAME:?word}\f[R] is a privacy\-preserving procmail\-rs
extension.
If \f[CR]NAME\f[R] is unset or empty, evaluation reports only
\f[CR]parameter NAME is unset or empty\f[R].
The parser checks \f[CR]word\f[R] syntax, but the evaluator neither
expands nor executes it and never includes it in the diagnostic.
\f[CR]word\f[R] may be empty.
.PP
\f[CR]${#NAME}\f[R] produces the length of the value in bytes, not
Unicode characters.
\f[CR]${NAME#pattern}\f[R] removes the shortest matching prefix and
\f[CR]${NAME##pattern}\f[R] removes the longest.
\f[CR]${NAME%pattern}\f[R] and \f[CR]${NAME%%pattern}\f[R] apply the
corresponding operations to a suffix.
If no prefix or suffix matches, the value is unchanged.
.PP
\f[CR]${NAME\(hapattern}\f[R] converts the first byte to ASCII uppercase
when that byte matches \f[CR]pattern\f[R];
\f[CR]${NAME\(ha\(hapattern}\f[R] applies the conversion to every
matching byte.
\f[CR]${NAME,pattern}\f[R] and \f[CR]${NAME,,pattern}\f[R] provide the
corresponding ASCII lowercase operations.
An omitted or empty pattern behaves as \f[CR]?\f[R], matching any single
byte.
These operations are byte\-oriented and locale\-independent: ASCII
letters change case and every non\-ASCII byte is preserved.
.PP
Patterns operate on bytes.
\f[CR]*\f[R] matches zero or more bytes, \f[CR]?\f[R] matches one byte,
and bracket expressions select bytes or inclusive byte ranges.
A leading \f[CR]!\f[R] or \f[CR]\(ha\f[R] negates a class.
Backslash quotes the next pattern byte.
An unterminated or empty bracket expression is treated as a literal
\f[CR][\f[R].
Pattern words use the normal variable, command, quote, and nesting
syntax.
Metacharacters from an unquoted variable or command remain active;
single quotes, double quotes, and backslash make their protected bytes
literal.
.PP
Pattern expansion shares the enclosing value limit.
Matching is non\-recursive and uses memory proportional to the bounded
pattern.
Work above 16777216 state transitions is rejected before matching
starts.
.IP
.EX
ARCHIVE=reports/2026/final.tar.gz
NAME=${ARCHIVE##*/}
STEM=${NAME%%.*}
BYTES=${#STEM}
LOWER=${STEM,,}
.EE
.PP
Inserted variable bytes are literal and are not scanned for another
expansion.
Outside quotes, backslash makes the next byte literal.
Inside double quotes, it quotes only \f[CR]$\f[R], backquote, double
quote, backslash, and newline; before another byte the backslash is
retained.
Inside single quotes, every byte is literal until the next single quote:
variables are not expanded, backquoted commands are not run, and
backslash has no special meaning.
.IP
.EX
MAILDIR=/srv/mail
FOLDER=${ACCOUNT:\-personal}
TAG=${OPTIONAL_TAG:=inbox}
LABEL=\(aqliteral $ACCOUNT\(aq\-\(dq\-$FOLDER\(dq

:0
maildir:$MAILDIR/$FOLDER/
.EE
.PP
Field splitting, pathname expansion, tilde expansion, arithmetic
substitution, special parameters, and other parameter operators are not
rc expansion features.
Shell command text is different: it is interpreted by the trusted shell
selected by \f[CR]SHELL\f[R] and \f[CR]SHELLFLAGS\f[R].
.SH ASSIGNMENTS
An ordinary assignment replaces the runtime value used by following
statements.
Top\-level settings that affect parsing, such as \f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R] and
the \f[CR]LIMIT_RC_*\f[R] family, apply only to following source text.
Parser\-limit assignments are rejected inside recipe blocks.
.PP
Backquotes run a trusted shell command when the assignment is reached.
The command receives the complete current message.
All trailing LF bytes are removed from its bounded stdout before
fragments are joined.
.IP
.EX
DOMAIN=\(gaprintf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(dq$MATCH1\(dq | tr A\-Z a\-z\(ga
ARCHIVE=${DOMAIN:\-unknown}
.EE
.PP
No partial value becomes visible if expansion, execution, or output
validation fails.
.SH RECIPES
The general form is:
.IP
.EX
:0 [FLAGS] [: [LOCKFILE]]
* CONDITION
* CONDITION
ACTION
.EE
.PP
All conditions must match.
With no conditions, the recipe matches immediately.
A delivering recipe stops processing the original message; a
\f[CR]c\f[R] recipe acts on a copy and permits later recipes to
continue.
.IP
.EX
:0
* \(haSubject:.*release
releases/

:0
inbox/
.EE
.SS Recipe flags
Condition flags and action flags are independent: uppercase \f[CR]H\f[R]
and \f[CR]B\f[R] select where conditions search, while lowercase
\f[CR]h\f[R] and \f[CR]b\f[R] select bytes passed to an action.
Repeating a flag has no additional effect.
.TP
\f[CR]H\f[R]
Search normalized headers.
This is the condition default when neither \f[CR]H\f[R] nor \f[CR]B\f[R]
is present.
Header folding is normalized only for matching; delivery retains the
original bytes.
.TP
\f[CR]B\f[R]
Search the body.
\f[CR]HB\f[R] or \f[CR]BH\f[R] searches one combined area containing
normalized headers, their separator, and the body.
.TP
\f[CR]D\f[R]
Distinguish ASCII uppercase and lowercase in regex conditions.
Matching is case\-insensitive by default.
\f[CR]\(haFROM_DAEMON\f[R] remains case\-insensitive because that
procmail macro requires it.
.TP
\f[CR]A\f[R]
Run only if the last preceding recipe at the same nesting level which
did not itself have \f[CR]A\f[R] or \f[CR]a\f[R] had matching
conditions.
This permits several actions to share one test.
.TP
\f[CR]a\f[R]
Like \f[CR]A\f[R], and additionally require the immediately preceding
recipe action to have succeeded.
.TP
\f[CR]E\f[R]
Run only if the immediately preceding recipe was not executed.
A selected \f[CR]E\f[R] recipe prevents a following \f[CR]E\f[R] recipe,
forming an else\-if chain.
.TP
\f[CR]e\f[R]
Run only if the immediately preceding recipe action was attempted and
failed.
A recipe skipped because its conditions did not match is not an error.
.TP
\f[CR]c\f[R]
Deliver or process a copy, then continue with later recipes.
A copy\-only result does not count as delivery of the original.
\f[CR]c\f[R] on a recipe block is rejected because process\-cloning
block behavior is not implemented.
.TP
\f[CR]h\f[R]
Pass only the header section to a pipe, filter, capture, or
sole\-\f[CR]|\f[R] stdout action.
.TP
\f[CR]b\f[R]
Pass only the body to one of those actions.
With neither or both of \f[CR]h\f[R] and \f[CR]b\f[R], pass the complete
message.
Partial\-area filesystem delivery is rejected.
.TP
\f[CR]f\f[R]
Treat a pipe as a filter.
Successful bounded stdout replaces the action area; unselected message
bytes are retained.
This flag requires a non\-empty pipe command.
.TP
\f[CR]w\f[R]
Wait for a child and make an ordinary nonzero status fail the action.
The failure is diagnosed and may select a following \f[CR]e\f[R] recipe.
.TP
\f[CR]W\f[R]
Apply the same status handling as \f[CR]w\f[R], but suppress the
child\-failure diagnostic.
\f[CR]w\f[R] and \f[CR]W\f[R] cannot be combined.
.PP
A non\-filter pipe without \f[CR]w\f[R] or \f[CR]W\f[R] continues recipe
evaluation after its complete selected input has been written.
The command remains supervised in the background: \f[CR]TIMEOUT\f[R],
process\-group termination, stderr redirection to the active
\f[CR]LOGFILE\f[R], reaping, and any local recipe lock remain in effect.
procmail\-rs reaps all such commands before finishing the message.
At most 128 background commands may belong to one message; the next
action fails instead of creating another process or worker.
.TP
\f[CR]i\f[R]
Ignore only an error encountered while writing selected input to a child
or direct stdout.
It does not ignore child status, timeout, invalid filter output, or
output\-limit failure.
It is rejected on filesystem delivery so a partial message cannot be
reported as delivered.
.TP
\f[CR]r\f[R]
Preserve the selected input ending for pipe\-like actions.
Without \f[CR]r\f[R], the action input receives procmail\-compatible
terminating LF bytes.
Maildir already preserves the message, while mbox retains its required
postmark and framing even with \f[CR]r\f[R].
.PP
Flags that have no meaning for an action are either rejected or, for the
documented block cases, ignored with a source\-located warning.
In particular, \f[CR]i\f[R], \f[CR]h\f[R], and \f[CR]b\f[R] cannot
weaken filesystem delivery.
.PP
This chain delivers command failures to a fallback Maildir:
.IP
.EX
:0 fw
| bogofilter \-p \-u \-e

:0 e
failed\-filter/

:0
* \(haX\-Bogosity: (Spam|Yes)
spam/
.EE
.PP
Uppercase chaining can reuse one condition without repeating its regex.
The lowercase \f[CR]a\f[R] additionally depends on successful completion
of the immediately preceding action:
.IP
.EX
:0 c
* \(haX\-Project: compiler
projects/compiler/

:0 Ac
mbox:project\-audit

:0 a
projects/notified/
.EE
.PP
An \f[CR]E\f[R] recipe supplies an else branch when the preceding recipe
was skipped:
.IP
.EX
:0
* \(haList\-Id:
lists/

:0 E
ordinary/
.EE
.SH CONDITIONS
.SS Regular expressions
A plain condition searches the area selected by the recipe.
Matching operates on bytes and ignores ASCII case unless \f[CR]D\f[R] is
present.
It is not Unicode matching: character classes, \f[CR].\f[R] and case
folding operate on message bytes.
Prefix \f[CR]!\f[R] negates any supported condition and repeated
\f[CR]!\f[R] prefixes alternate the result.
.IP
.EX
:0 D
* ! \(haX\-Verified: yes$
unverified/
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]H ?? REGEX\f[R], \f[CR]B ?? REGEX\f[R], and \f[CR]HB ?? REGEX\f[R]
or \f[CR]BH ?? REGEX\f[R] override the recipe search area for one
condition.
\f[CR]$NAME ?? REGEX\f[R] searches the current byte value of a variable.
.IP
.EX
:0 B
* H ?? \(haContent\-Type: text/plain
* urgent
urgent\-body/
.EE
.SS Structured address and identifier conditions
The \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R] \f[CR]address\f[R] condition extracts mailbox
addresses from one or more address\-bearing fields and applies
\f[CR]REGEX\f[R] separately to every resulting address:
.IP
.EX
:0
* address From,Sender,Reply\-To ?? \(haalerts(?:\(rs+[\(ha\(at]+)?\(atexample\(rs.org$
alerts/
.EE
.PP
Its syntax is \f[CR]address FIELD[,FIELD...] ?? REGEX\f[R].
The supported field names are \f[CR]From\f[R], \f[CR]To\f[R],
\f[CR]Cc\f[R], \f[CR]Sender\f[R], and \f[CR]Reply\-To\f[R]; names are
ASCII case\-insensitive and repetitions are rejected.
Display names, comments, groups, angle brackets, folding whitespace, and
separators are not presented to the regex.
The local part retains its spelling and case, while ASCII domain letters
are converted to lowercase.
A quoted local part remains quoted.
Malformed mailbox elements are ignored, so text resembling an address in
a display name cannot accidentally become the match input.
At most 4096 valid mailboxes from the selected fields are tested for one
condition.
.PP
\f[CR]List\-Id\f[R] is not a mailbox field.
Use the separate syntax \f[CR]identifier List\-Id ?? REGEX\f[R]; the
regex receives only the identifier between angle brackets, with ASCII
letters converted to lowercase:
.IP
.EX
:0
* identifier List\-Id ?? \(halinux\-kernel\(rs.vger\(rs.kernel\(rs.org$
lists/linux\-kernel/
.EE
.PP
Both forms require only the header section and stop at the first
matching value.
Their regex groups and \f[CR]\(rs/\f[R] marker update \f[CR]MATCH\f[R],
\f[CR]MATCH1\f[R], and later numbered values in the same way as other
regex conditions.
The leading keywords do not reserve variable names:
\f[CR]address ?? REGEX\f[R] and \f[CR]identifier ?? REGEX\f[R] remain
ordinary variable conditions.
.PP
The ordinary operators are:
.TP
\f[CR]literal\f[R]
Match the same literal byte sequence.
A backslash quotes a regex metabyte.
.TP
\f[CR].\f[R]
Match one byte other than LF.
.TP
\f[CR][abc]\f[R], \f[CR][\(haabc]\f[R], \f[CR][a\-z]\f[R], \f[CR][[:digit:]]\f[R]
Match one listed, excluded, ranged, or named ASCII byte class.
.TP
\f[CR]a?\f[R], \f[CR]a*\f[R], \f[CR]a+\f[R]
Match zero or one, zero or more, or one or more repetitions.
.TP
\f[CR]a{m}\f[R], \f[CR]a{m,}\f[R], \f[CR]a{m,n}\f[R]
Counted repetition in the Rust regex dialect.
Original procmail treats braces as ordinary text; escape them as
\f[CR]\(rs{\f[R] and \f[CR]\(rs}\f[R] when literal braces are intended.
.TP
\f[CR]ab|cd\f[R], \f[CR](expression)\f[R]
Alternation and grouping.
Capturing groups populate numbered \f[CR]MATCHn\f[R] variables after a
successful condition.
Noncapturing groups \f[CR](?:...)\f[R] are accepted.
Named groups, look\-around, and backreferences are rejected.
.PP
The dialect also accepts ASCII \f[CR]\(rsd\f[R], \f[CR]\(rss\f[R],
\f[CR]\(rsw\f[R] classes and their uppercase negations, zero\-width
\f[CR]\(rsb\f[R] and \f[CR]\(rsB\f[R], hexadecimal byte escapes, lazy
repetition, and scoped flag groups such as \f[CR](?i:expression)\f[R].
Some of these forms have no equivalent in original procmail.
Unicode properties are unavailable because matching operates on bytes.
.PP
The following anchors and macros have procmail\-specific handling:
.TP
\f[CR]\(ha\f[R], \f[CR]$\f[R]
Match the start or end of a line or the edge of the selected search
area.
At an internal line boundary they consume the separating LF, matching
the documented procmail multiline behavior.
.TP
\f[CR]\(ha\(ha\f[R]
At the start of an expression, match only the start of the complete
selected area.
At the end, match only its end.
\f[CR]\(ha\(ha\f[R] elsewhere is rejected.
.TP
\f[CR]\(rs<\f[R], \f[CR]\(rs>\f[R]
Match and consume one byte outside \f[CR][A\-Za\-z0\-9_]\f[R], including
LF.
These are procmail consuming word\-edge forms, not zero\-width word
boundaries.
.TP
\f[CR]\(rs/\f[R]
Mark the start of the suffix assigned to \f[CR]MATCH\f[R].
Up to 64 \f[CR]\(rs/\f[R] markers may occur in one expression, including
inside groups and alternatives.
The last marker reached by the successful path selects the value; a
successful alternative which does not reach any marker preserves the
preceding \f[CR]MATCH\f[R].
The value extends to the end of the complete regex match, so it includes
an LF consumed by a terminal \f[CR]$\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\(haTO\f[R], \f[CR]\(haTO_\f[R]
Expand to procmail\-compatible recipient\-header expressions.
\f[CR]\(haTO\f[R] uses a word\-style address boundary;
\f[CR]\(haTO_\f[R] uses the broader address boundary.
The macro is recognized wherever it occurs unless its caret is
immediately preceded by backslash.
.TP
\f[CR]\(haFROM_DAEMON\f[R], \f[CR]\(haFROM_MAILER\f[R]
Expand to the fixed procmail daemon or mailer\-sender expressions.
\f[CR]\(haFROM_DAEMON\f[R] forces case\-insensitive matching.
.PP
Text matched after \f[CR]\(rs/\f[R] is assigned to \f[CR]MATCH\f[R];
ordinary capture groups are assigned to \f[CR]MATCH1\f[R],
\f[CR]MATCH2\f[R], and so on, excluding the private groups used to
locate \f[CR]MATCH\f[R] markers.
An unmatched optional group becomes an empty value.
At most 64 ordinary capture groups are accepted and one captured value
is bounded to 64 KiB.
.IP
.EX
:0 c
* \(haList\-Id:.*<\(rs/([\(ha.>]+)\(rs.([\(ha.>]+)>
maildir:lists/$MATCH2/$MATCH1/
.EE
.PP
The complete user pattern is bounded to 64 KiB, the parsed regex tree to
256 levels, and its compiled form to 8 MiB.
Generated text for the four fixed macros does not consume
\f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R], but does consume the translated\-pattern and
compiled\-size ceilings.
.PP
Procmail chooses the leftmost shortest match except while selecting
\f[CR]MATCH\f[R].
For a condition containing \f[CR]\(rs/\f[R], it selects the leftmost
shortest span before the reached marker and the leftmost longest suffix
after it.
The \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R] engine is leftmost\-first, so ambiguous
alternatives and repetitions can select different spans, marker paths,
and numbered captures.
Alternative order therefore matters in \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R] even where
it did not affect original procmail.
Weighted scoring conditions and their \f[CR]$=\f[R] result are rejected.
See the compatibility document before migrating expressions that depend
on ambiguous match selection.
.IP
.EX
:0
* \(haSubject: [[:digit:]]{3}$
numbered\-subject/
.EE
.SS Size conditions
\f[CR]< NUMBER\f[R] matches when the complete message is smaller than
the decimal byte count.
\f[CR]> NUMBER\f[R] matches when it is larger.
.IP
.EX
:0
* > 10485760
large/
.EE
.SS Program conditions
\f[CR]? COMMAND\f[R] sends the complete current message to a trusted
shell command and matches status zero.
\f[CR]TIMEOUT\f[R], \f[CR]LOGFILE\f[R], and the rebuilt bounded child
environment apply.
.IP
.EX
:0
* ? test \-e \(dq$HOME/.accept\-mail\(dq
accepted/
.EE
.SS Shell\-expanded conditions
A condition beginning with \f[CR]$\f[R] expands its remainder using the
supported double\-quoted rules and reparses the bounded result as a
condition.
\f[CR]$\(rsNAME\f[R] quotes the selected variable as literal regex
bytes, preventing its contents from becoming condition syntax.
.IP
.EX
ADDRESS=user\(atexample.org

:0
* $\(haTo:.*<$\(rsADDRESS>
addressed/
.EE
.PP
A backquoted command in this form receives the complete current message.
Every intermediate expansion remains subject to \f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R] and
the destination condition\(cqs own limits.
.SH ACTIONS
.SS Maildir and mbox
\f[CR]maildir:PATH\f[R] and a path ending in \f[CR]/\f[R] select
Maildir.
\f[CR]mbox:PATH\f[R] and every other unmarked path select mboxrd.
The unmarked path \f[CR]/dev/null\f[R] is the sole discard action.
Backend selection never depends on the current filesystem type.
.PP
Relative paths use \f[CR]MAILDIR\f[R] active when the statement
executes, or the process working directory when it is unset.
.IP
.EX
MAILDIR=/srv/mail

:0 c
maildir:audit/

:0
mbox:archive/received.mbox
.EE
.PP
Maildir directories must already contain \f[CR]tmp\f[R], \f[CR]new\f[R],
and \f[CR]cur\f[R].
Filesystem paths reject NUL, empty components, \f[CR].\f[R],
\f[CR]..\f[R], repeated separators, and unexpected trailing separators.
.PP
Linux keeps pending Maildir data in an unnamed temporary inode.
FreeBSD uses exclusive named creation in \f[CR]tmp\f[R] and therefore
also requires the Maildir and its three subdirectories to be owned by
the current uid and not writable by group or other users.
It checks the open file identity before publication and cleanup, but a
hostile process with directory mutation access could still win a race
between a check and a pathname operation.
.SS Pipes and filters
An action beginning with \f[CR]|\f[R] runs its remainder through the
configured trusted shell.
The selected message bytes are written to stdin.
A successful \f[CR]f\f[R] recipe replaces the selected message area with
bounded stdout.
.IP
.EX
:0 fw
| formail \-I \(aqX\-Checked: yes\(aq
.EE
.PP
A complete action consisting only of \f[CR]|\f[R] writes the selected
area directly to \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R] standard output without starting
a shell.
.SS Command\-output capture
\f[CR]NAME=| COMMAND\f[R] assigns bounded stdout.
Exactly one trailing LF is removed.
The \f[CR]h\f[R] and \f[CR]b\f[R] flags choose the input area, while
\f[CR]w\f[R], \f[CR]W\f[R], \f[CR]i\f[R], and \f[CR]r\f[R] retain their
pipe meanings.
.IP
.EX
:0 hW
MSG_FROM=| sed \-n \(aqs/\(haFrom: //p\(aq
.EE
.SS Recipe blocks
A matched block evaluates its child statements in order.
Chain state is local to the block.
A local lock named on the block is held across the complete child
sequence.
.IP
.EX
:0 : account.lock
{
    ACCOUNT=work

    :0
    * \(haX\-Account: work
    work/
}
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]c\f[R] flag clones a block branch.
The parent skips the block and continues after it, while the branch
executes the block and then follows the same remaining recipes unless a
delivering action stops that branch.
Variable assignments, message changes, global locks, and runtime rc
transitions remain local to each side.
Runtime include and switch paths are resolved from the variables of the
side which reaches them.
.PP
A plain \f[CR]c\f[R] branch runs concurrently with its parent.
procmail\-rs supervises and joins every branch before message processing
returns.
An ordinary action failure in that branch does not change the parent
status, while configuration, resource\-limit, and supervision failures
still fail the message.
At most 128 background copy branches may be started for one message;
reaching the limit fails evaluation.
The \f[CR]cw\f[R] and \f[CR]cW\f[R] combinations wait before the parent
continues and apply the branch status.
Naming a local lock on a copy block also makes it waited, as in original
procmail, so the lock covers the complete branch execution.
.SS Native header editing
The \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R] extension \f[CR]headers { ... }\f[R] changes
the current header without starting an external filter.
It is a recipe action, not a top\-level statement and not standard
procmail syntax:
.IP
.EX
:0 [CONDITION_FLAGS] [CONTROL_FLAG]
* CONDITION
headers {
    OPERATION
    ...
}
.EE
.PP
The opening brace must occur on the action line after the word
\f[CR]headers\f[R].
Blank and comment\-only lines are accepted between operations.
The closing \f[CR]}\f[R] must appear alone apart from whitespace.
One action may contain at most 256 operations, and every physical
operation line is checked against the active \f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R].
.PP
Operations execute from top to bottom.
A later operation sees the result of every earlier one; this matters
when \f[CR]set\f[R], \f[CR]add\f[R], and \f[CR]remove\f[R] use the same
field name.
Field\-name comparisons ignore ASCII case, while field values are never
interpreted as addresses or MIME text.
.TP
\f[CR]remove NAME\f[R]
Delete every field named \f[CR]NAME\f[R].
A field includes all immediately following physical lines beginning with
space or tab, so removing a folded field also removes all its
continuation lines.
Absence of the field is success.
.IP
.EX
headers {
    remove X\-Spam\-Status
}
.EE
.TP
\f[CR]set NAME [VALUE]\f[R]
Replace the first matching field at its existing position and remove
every later duplicate.
If no match exists, append one new field.
The emitted name uses the spelling supplied by this operation, even when
it replaces a field with different capitalization.
\f[CR]VALUE\f[R] may be empty.
.IP
.EX
headers {
    set X\-Spam\-Status checked
    set X\-Empty
}
.EE
.TP
\f[CR]add NAME [VALUE]\f[R]
Append one field after all fields currently present.
Existing fields with that name are retained, so repeated \f[CR]add\f[R]
operations deliberately create repeated fields in source order.
.IP
.EX
headers {
    add Received by first\-filter
    add Received by second\-filter
}
.EE
.TP
\f[CR]prepend NAME [VALUE]\f[R]
Insert one field before every field currently present.
Consequently, a later \f[CR]prepend\f[R] appears before an earlier
\f[CR]prepend\f[R].
.IP
.EX
headers {
    prepend X\-Processed\-By first
    prepend X\-Processed\-By second
}
.EE
.PP
Here the resulting order is \f[CR]second\f[R], then \f[CR]first\f[R],
then the original fields.
.TP
\f[CR]rename OLD\-NAME to NEW\-NAME\f[R]
Rename every matching field without changing its value, continuation
lines, position, or line endings.
Matching \f[CR]OLD\-NAME\f[R] ignores ASCII case, and all renamed fields
use the stable ASCII title case described below.
.IP
.EX
headers {
    rename X\-Old\-Status to X\-Status
}
.EE
.TP
\f[CR]extract raw NAME into VARIABLE\f[R]
Assign the value of the first matching field to an ordinary user
variable.
The field name, colon, and final line ending are omitted; leading
whitespace, embedded fold line endings, continuation indentation, and
arbitrary bytes are preserved.
An absent field assigns an empty value.
.TP
\f[CR]extract unfolded NAME into VARIABLE\f[R]
Extract the first matching field after removing leading space and tab
from each physical value line and joining continuation lines with one
ASCII space.
It does not decode MIME words, parse addresses, or interpret the
resulting bytes as UTF\-8.
.TP
\f[CR]extract decoded NAME into VARIABLE\f[R]
Unfold the first matching value and decode RFC 2047 \f[CR]B\f[R] and
\f[CR]Q\f[R] encoded\-words.
Adjacent encoded\-words are joined without their separating whitespace.
\f[CR]UTF\-8\f[R], \f[CR]US\-ASCII\f[R], and \f[CR]ISO\-8859\-1\f[R]
charset names are accepted without regard to ASCII case; every other
charset is rejected.
Plain text and the final result must be valid UTF\-8.
A malformed encoded\-word, unsupported transfer encoding, control
character, or result exceeding \f[CR]MAX_ASSIGNMENT_VALUE_LEN\f[R] fails
the complete \f[CR]headers\f[R] action.
Decoding is performed once and the result is not interpreted as rc
syntax.
.IP
.EX
headers {
    extract unfolded To into MSG_TO
    extract decoded Subject into MSG_SUBJECT
    extract raw X\-Original into ORIGINAL_RAW
}
.EE
.PP
The decoded value can be matched explicitly without changing the raw
header matching behavior:
.IP
.EX
:0
headers {
    extract decoded Subject into MSG_SUBJECT
}

:0
* MSG_SUBJECT ?? Scholar.*
maildir:scholar/
.EE
.PP
Extraction can assign only an ordinary user variable; message data
cannot change settings, limits, or runtime\-produced names.
Extracted values become visible after the complete action succeeds, so
later recipes and runtime rc files may use them.
A later failure in the same action publishes none of its extractions.
Repeated extraction into one variable follows operation order, with the
last value winning.
.PP
\f[CR]NAME\f[R] must be non\-empty printable ASCII without whitespace or
colon, matching the \f[CR]1*ftext\f[R] field\-name syntax from RFC 5322.
Punctuation allowed by that syntax is preserved.
The first ASCII whitespace separates \f[CR]NAME\f[R] from
\f[CR]VALUE\f[R]; colons in \f[CR]VALUE\f[R] are ordinary data:
.IP
.EX
headers {
    set X\-Source\-URI imap://mail.example/inbox
}
.EE
.PP
Leading whitespace before \f[CR]VALUE\f[R] is removed by the rc parser.
Names emitted by \f[CR]set\f[R], \f[CR]add\f[R], \f[CR]prepend\f[R], and
\f[CR]rename\f[R] use stable ASCII title case: the first letter and each
letter after \f[CR]\-\f[R] is uppercase, while other ASCII letters are
lowercase.
Thus \f[CR]x\-SPAM\-status\f[R] is emitted as
\f[CR]X\-Spam\-Status\f[R].
Existing fields that are not replaced or renamed retain their original
spelling.
.PP
Printable US\-ASCII, space, and horizontal tab in \f[CR]VALUE\f[R] are
emitted without encoding.
If \f[CR]VALUE\f[R] contains non\-ASCII Unicode, procmail\-rs encodes
the whole value as one or more RFC 2047 \f[CR]UTF\-8/B\f[R]
encoded\-words, splits only at UTF\-8 character boundaries, and folds
between words.
This is intended primarily for unstructured fields such as
\f[CR]Subject\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
headers {
    set Subject Привет
}
.EE
.PP
becomes \f[CR]Subject: =?UTF\-8?B?0J/RgNC40LLQtdGC?=\f[R].
Automatic encoding is a deliberate convenience compromise: RFC 2047
encoded\-words are not valid in every position of every structured
field.
The action does not classify field types, so the configuration author
remains responsible for choosing a field where encoded\-words are
permitted.
An already encoded ASCII value is emitted unchanged.
NUL and other control characters are rejected rather than encoded.
.PP
An unencoded generated physical field line may not exceed 998 bytes
excluding its line ending.
RFC 2047 encoded\-words are limited to 75 characters and lines
containing them to 76 characters.
.PP
New fields are serialized as \f[CR]NAME: VALUE\f[R] followed by the line
ending chosen from the first physical input header line.
If the input has no such line, the header/body separator selects CRLF or
LF; LF is the final fallback.
Existing unmodified fields, their original endings, malformed binary
fields, the separator, and the body retain their bytes.
.PP
\f[CR]VALUE\f[R] supports all parameter forms described under VARIABLE
EXPANSION and the ordinary backslash rules described there.
Expansion occurs when the selected recipe executes, so \f[CR]MATCH\f[R],
numbered captures, \f[CR]LASTFOLDER\f[R], and earlier assignments may be
used:
.IP
.EX
:0
* \(haList\-Id:.*<\(rs/([\(ha.>]+)\(rs.example>
headers {
    set X\-List\-Name $MATCH1
}
.EE
.PP
Expanded bytes are inserted literally and are not scanned a second time.
Backquoted commands are not accepted in header\-operation values.
NUL, CR, LF, and a trailing backslash requesting a folded continuation
are rejected; native editing cannot create a multi\-line field.
.PP
The condition flags \f[CR]H\f[R], \f[CR]B\f[R], and \f[CR]D\f[R],
control flags \f[CR]A\f[R], \f[CR]a\f[R], \f[CR]E\f[R], and
\f[CR]e\f[R], and \f[CR]c\f[R] are accepted.
A successful native edit always continues with the next recipe, so
\f[CR]c\f[R] has no additional effect.
Flags \f[CR]h\f[R], \f[CR]b\f[R], \f[CR]f\f[R], \f[CR]w\f[R],
\f[CR]W\f[R], \f[CR]i\f[R], and \f[CR]r\f[R] are rejected because this
action neither selects pipe input nor invokes a child.
A local lockfile is also rejected because no external publication
occurs.
.PP
Every operation is checked against \f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_SIZE\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_HEADERS\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_LINE\f[R], and
\f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_FIELD\f[R].
All operations are prepared privately; if parsing, expansion,
validation, or any size check fails, none of the changes becomes the
current message and the preceding message remains available to normal
error handling.
A successful header\-only execution can still stream an unread body
without retaining it in memory.
.PP
Each extracted value is independently limited to
\f[CR]MAX_ASSIGNMENT_VALUE_LEN\f[R] (65536 bytes).
The limit is checked while unfolding, before an oversized result can be
accumulated.
Raw extraction checks the selected byte range before copying it.
Together with the 256\-operation limit, this also caps values retained
by one action at 16 MiB.
.PP
For example, several common \f[CR]formail \-I\f[R] cleanup operations
can be expressed without copying the message through a child:
.IP
.EX
:0
headers {
    remove X\-Spam\-Status
    set X\-Filter checked
    add X\-Filter\-Result clean
    prepend X\-Processed\-By procmail\-rs
}
.EE
.PP
This extension is not a general replacement for \f[B]formail\f[R].
It does not parse addresses, decode MIME fields, generate
\f[CR]Message\-Id\f[R], split digests, change the body, or implement
\f[CR]formail\f[R]\(cqs duplicate\-detection and reply\-header modes.
Use a trusted pipe when those operations are required.
.SH VARIABLE REFERENCE
Names outside the typed names and unsupported list below are user
variables.
They can be assigned in an rc file or supplied initially with
\f[B]\(enset\f[R].
Values may be arbitrary bounded bytes when produced by a capture, but a
value exported to a child environment cannot contain NUL.
Assignment and expansion occur in statement order.
.SS Positional parameters
Repeatable \f[B]\-a\f[R] or \f[B]\(enargument\f[R] command\-line options
initialize \f[CR]$1\f[R], \f[CR]$2\f[R], and later numbered parameters.
\f[CR]${N}\f[R] is the braced form; \f[CR]$10\f[R] denotes the tenth
parameter.
\f[CR]$#\f[R] and \f[CR]${#}\f[R] contain the number of supplied
arguments.
A permitted but absent numbered parameter expands to an empty string.
.IP
.EX
procmail\-rs filter \-\-config rules.rc \-a work \-a /home/user/Mail/work
.EE
.IP
.EX
ACCOUNT=$1

:0
* ACCOUNT ?? \(hawork$
maildir:${2}/
.EE
.PP
At most 256 arguments are accepted, with a 64 KiB limit on one value and
a 256 KiB combined limit.
Their text is inserted literally, remains available to runtime
\f[CR]INCLUDERC\f[R] and \f[CR]SWITCHRC\f[R] files, and is not exported
under numeric child\-environment names.
\f[CR]$\(at\f[R] and \f[CR]$*\f[R] are unsupported.
.TP
\f[CR]SHIFT\f[R]
Assign a positive decimal integer to discard that many leading
positional arguments.
An amount larger than \f[CR]$#\f[R] discards all remaining arguments.
Following statements and runtime rc files observe the new \f[CR]$1\f[R]
and \f[CR]$#\f[R].
A copied recipe branch changes only its private argument window.
.IP
.EX
ACCOUNT=$1
SHIFT=1
FOLDER=$1
.EE
.PP
Unlike original procmail, zero, negative, and malformed values are
errors instead of silent no\-ops.
.SS Identity and runtime results
.TP
\f[CR]HOME\f[R]
Initialized from the current uid\(cqs passwd entry.
It is not imported from the ambient environment and may be replaced by
an rc assignment or \f[B]\(enset\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]LOGNAME\f[R]
Initialized and controlled like \f[CR]HOME\f[R] using the passwd login
name.
.TP
\f[CR]HOST\f[R]
Initialized from the system node name.
Assigning the exact system name continues processing.
Any other value ends the current rc file successfully; a bare
\f[CR]HOST\f[R] is the supported empty assignment and therefore also
ends it.
.TP
\f[CR]PROCMAIL_VERSION\f[R]
Read\-only \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R] package version.
It deliberately does not claim to contain the procmail 3.22 version.
.TP
\f[CR]LASTFOLDER\f[R]
Runtime\-only path of the last successfully published destination.
A failure before publication does not change it.
When an error occurs after a message became visible, it records that
visible destination.
.TP
\f[CR]MATCH\f[R]
Runtime\-only bytes selected by the most recent successful
\f[CR]\(rs/\f[R] regex marker.
Evaluating another condition which contains \f[CR]\(rs/\f[R] or ordinary
capture groups first clears all preceding match values, including when
that condition fails.
A regex without captures does not change them.
.TP
\f[CR]MATCH1\f[R], \f[CR]MATCH2\f[R], \&...
\f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R] additions containing ordinary regex capture
groups from the most recent successful regex condition.
Unmatched optional groups are empty.
These names cannot be assigned directly.
.SS Paths and delivery
.TP
\f[CR]MAILDIR\f[R]
Base directory for following relative destination, staging, lock,
include, switch, and log paths.
It has no implicit default; when unset or empty, relative paths use the
process working directory.
Every external command is started with the active non\-empty
\f[CR]MAILDIR\f[R] as its working directory.
The parent process directory is left unchanged so concurrent copy
branches can use different values safely.
.TP
\f[CR]DURABILITY\f[R]
Select \f[CR]none\f[R] (default), \f[CR]file\f[R], or \f[CR]full\f[R].
\f[CR]file\f[R] synchronizes delivered message data before success.
\f[CR]full\f[R] additionally synchronizes the documented publication
directories.
This is a statically prepared root setting rather than a
message\-conditional assignment.
.TP
\f[CR]UMASK\f[R]
Octal \f[CR]0000\f[R] through \f[CR]0777\f[R], default \f[CR]077\f[R].
It can only remove permission bits from restrictive modes requested for
newly created delivery, lock, and log files.
It never broadens permissions or changes the process\-wide umask.
.SS Locking
.TP
\f[CR]LOCKMETHOD\f[R]
\f[CR]flock\f[R] (default) selects a persistent checked file and kernel
lock.
\f[CR]dotlock\f[R] selects compatible exclusive creation, stale removal,
and final pathname removal with the documented replacement risk.
.TP
\f[CR]LOCKFILE\f[R]
Acquire a global lock at this statement.
A later assignment releases the previous lock before acquiring its
replacement; an empty value only releases it.
The active \f[CR]LOCKMETHOD\f[R], \f[CR]LOCKSLEEP\f[R],
\f[CR]LOCKTIMEOUT\f[R], \f[CR]UMASK\f[R], and \f[CR]MAILDIR\f[R] apply.
.TP
\f[CR]LOCKEXT\f[R]
Suffix used to derive an implicit local lock name, default
\f[CR].lock\f[R].
It may be empty but cannot contain NUL or \f[CR]/\f[R]; both the suffix
and resulting path are bounded.
.TP
\f[CR]LOCKTIMEOUT\f[R]
Finite lock acquisition limit in seconds, default 1024.
Decimal values 1 through 86400 are accepted.
Zero is rejected rather than meaning an unlimited wait.
.TP
\f[CR]LOCKSLEEP\f[R]
Retry interval in seconds for local, global, and mbox locks, default 8.
Decimal values 1 through 86400 are accepted.
Zero is rejected to prevent an active retry loop.
Assignments affect only later lock attempts.
.SS External processes
.TP
\f[CR]SHELL\f[R]
Absolute shell path used for every trusted command, default
\f[CR]/bin/sh\f[R].
Empty, relative, trailing\-slash, repeated\-component, \f[CR].\f[R] and
\f[CR]..\f[R] paths are rejected.
It may be supplied with \f[B]\(enset\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]SHELLFLAGS\f[R]
One argument placed between \f[CR]SHELL\f[R] and command text, default
\f[CR]\-c\f[R].
It is not split into several arguments and may be supplied with
\f[B]\(enset\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]PATH\f[R]
Child command search path, default \f[CR]/usr/bin:/bin\f[R].
It belongs to the rebuilt child environment and may be supplied with
\f[B]\(enset\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]TIMEOUT\f[R]
Finite child limit in seconds, default 960.
Decimal values 1 through 86400 are accepted.
It covers concurrent stdin writing, stdout reading, waiting,
process\-group termination, and reaping.
.TP
\f[CR]TRAP\f[R]
Bounded trusted shell command run after normal recipe completion and
complete input validation.
The final message plus one LF is supplied on stdin; stdout and stderr go
to \f[CR]LOGFILE\f[R].
An empty assignment disables a preceding trap.
.TP
\f[CR]EXITCODE\f[R]
Final decimal status from 0 through 255.
An explicit non\-empty value takes precedence.
An explicitly empty value allows a nonzero \f[CR]TRAP\f[R] result to
become the final status; when never assigned, the provisional filter
result remains authoritative and is exported to the trap.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
\f[CR]LOGFILE\f[R]
Append destination for trace records, diagnostics, and child stderr.
Empty or unset means diagnostics use standard error.
The path is resolved through the active \f[CR]MAILDIR\f[R].
This is a statically prepared root setting.
.TP
\f[CR]VERBOSE\f[R]
Enable or disable detailed tracing.
Case\-insensitive prefixes \f[CR]on\f[R], \f[CR]y\f[R], \f[CR]t\f[R],
\f[CR]e\f[R], or a nonzero leading digit mean true; \f[CR]off\f[R],
\f[CR]n\f[R], \f[CR]f\f[R], \f[CR]d\f[R], or a leading zero mean false.
It is disabled by default and is statically prepared.
.TP
\f[CR]LOGDETAIL\f[R]
\f[CR]metadata\f[R] (default) prevents variable values from entering
trace records.
\f[CR]values\f[R] permits bounded and escaped value prefixes.
Neither mode permits bodies, header values, regex text, destination
paths, or command arguments.
.TP
\f[CR]LOGABSTRACT\f[R]
Only \f[CR]no\f[R] is accepted.
Original abstract modes are rejected because they log sensitive
\f[CR]From\f[R] and \f[CR]Subject\f[R] values.
No abstract is generated by default.
.SS Input and parser limits
.TP
\f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R]
Decimal limit for following physical rc lines, complete continued pipe
commands, and values expanded by \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R].
It defaults to 2048 and may range from 128 through 1048576.
Only a literal top\-level assignment is accepted.
.PP
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_SIZE\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_HEADERS\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_BODY\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_LINE\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_FIELD\f[R]
.PP
: Bound complete mail, the header section, body, one physical header
line, and one logical folded field.
Values accept binary \f[CR]K\f[R], \f[CR]M\f[R], and \f[CR]G\f[R]
suffixes and cannot exceed the ceilings listed under LIMITS.
These are statically prepared root settings.
.PP
\f[CR]LIMIT_MAX_ASSIGNMENTS\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_RC_STATEMENTS\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_RECIPES\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_RC_CONDITIONS\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_REGEXES\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_RECIPE_CONDITIONS\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_RECIPE_NESTING\f[R]
.PP
: Decimal structural limits applied to following syntax.
The assignment that changes a limit is checked using the preceding
limit.
These assignments and \f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R] are rejected inside recipe
blocks.
.SS Explicitly unsupported reserved names
Assignments and references to \f[CR]DEFAULT\f[R], \f[CR]ORGMAIL\f[R],
\f[CR]COMSAT\f[R], \f[CR]DELIVERED\f[R], \f[CR]DROPPRIVS\f[R],
\f[CR]LOG\f[R], \f[CR]MSGPREFIX\f[R], \f[CR]NORESRETRY\f[R],
\f[CR]PROCMAIL_OVERFLOW\f[R], \f[CR]SHELLMETAS\f[R], \f[CR]SUSPEND\f[R],
\f[CR]SENDMAIL\f[R], and \f[CR]SENDMAILFLAGS\f[R] are rejected with an
explicit unsupported\-variable diagnostic.
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_SIZE\f[R] is likewise reserved and rejected because an rc
file cannot choose the bound under which that same file was already
read.
These names are not treated as ordinary user variables.
.SH RUNTIME RC FILES
\f[CR]INCLUDERC=EXPRESSION\f[R] evaluates another rc file and then
returns to the caller.
\f[CR]SWITCHRC=EXPRESSION\f[R] evaluates another file and abandons the
remainder of the current file; an empty value ends only the current
file.
A value resolving exactly to \f[CR]/dev/null\f[R] also ends the current
file, consumes one runtime rc transition, and does not open the device.
This special case does not apply to \f[CR]INCLUDERC\f[R], which retains
the regular\-file checks.
.IP
.EX
:0
* \(haList\-Id:.*<\(rs/[\(ha.>]+\(rs.([\(ha.>]+)>
{
    INCLUDERC=$MAILDIR/rules/$MATCH1.rc
}
.EE
.PP
Runtime files must be regular, owned by the owner of the root rc file,
neither group\- nor other\-writable, and not reached through a
final\-component symlink.
Their number, total bytes, nesting depth, and transitions are bounded.
.SH LOCKING AND DURABILITY
A recipe colon requests a local lock.
With no explicit name, a filesystem recipe derives it by appending
\f[CR]LOCKEXT\f[R] to the resolved destination.
An explicit name is required for a pipe or block.
.IP
.EX
LOCKMETHOD=flock
LOCKSLEEP=2
LOCKTIMEOUT=30

:0 : archive.lock
mbox:archive
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]LOCKMETHOD=flock\f[R] is the default and retains a checked lock
file after use.
\f[CR]LOCKMETHOD=dotlock\f[R] selects pathname\-based
procmail\-compatible locking and its documented replacement risk.
\f[CR]LOCKFILE\f[R] holds a global lock until it is replaced, cleared,
or processing ends.
.PP
During migration, \f[CR]LOCKMETHOD=dotlock\f[R] and a recipe colon
combine both locking schemes for an mbox.
The local lock is acquired first and the mbox \f[CR]flock\f[R] second;
they are released in reverse order.
This coordinates with old writers which use the same destination plus
\f[CR]LOCKEXT\f[R] and with new writers which lock the mbox itself:
.IP
.EX
LOCKMETHOD=dotlock

:0 :
mbox:archive
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]DURABILITY=none\f[R], \f[CR]file\f[R], or \f[CR]full\f[R] selects
publication sync points.
Maildir uses atomic no\-replace publication: Linux moves an unnamed
temporary inode through \f[CR]tmp\f[R], while FreeBSD links an
exclusively created named \f[CR]tmp\f[R] file into \f[CR]new\f[R].
Mbox holds \f[CR]flock\f[R], records the original length, and attempts
rollback after a failed append or sync.
No NFS\-safety claim is made.
.SH LOGGING
\f[CR]LOGFILE=PATH\f[R] selects append\-only diagnostics.
\f[CR]VERBOSE=yes\f[R] enables detailed metadata events without message
contents, header values, paths, regex text, commands, or variable
values.
\f[CR]LOGDETAIL=values\f[R] additionally permits bounded, escaped
variable value prefixes.
\f[CR]LOGABSTRACT=no\f[R] is accepted; header\-bearing abstract modes
are not.
.PP
Successful native header blocks log each operation in source order.
Records identify the operation, source line, header name, rename target,
or extraction variable as applicable.
They never contain a header value.
A variable assigned by \f[CR]extract\f[R] is recorded with its value
hidden even under \f[CR]LOGDETAIL=values\f[R].
.IP
.EX
LOGFILE=/srv/mail/filter.log
VERBOSE=yes
LOGDETAIL=values
.EE
.PP
Child stderr is appended to the active \f[CR]LOGFILE\f[R] and is not
discarded.
A trace\-write failure is reported once to stderr and does not turn a
successful delivery into a retry that could duplicate mail.
.SH PROCESS SETTINGS AND TRAP
\f[CR]SHELL\f[R], \f[CR]SHELLFLAGS\f[R], and \f[CR]PATH\f[R] construct
the trusted child environment.
Ambient environment entries are not inherited.
\f[CR]TIMEOUT\f[R] bounds every external shell; timeout terminates its
process group with \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R] followed by \f[CR]SIGKILL\f[R] and
reaps the direct child.
.PP
\f[CR]TRAP\f[R] is the last executed non\-empty trusted command assigned
in statement order.
It runs after complete input validation and recipe processing, receives
the final message plus one LF, and writes stdout and stderr to
\f[CR]LOGFILE\f[R].
It is not run after \f[CR]SIGHUP\f[R], \f[CR]SIGINT\f[R],
\f[CR]SIGQUIT\f[R], or \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R] interrupts filtering.
.IP
.EX
TIMEOUT=60
TRAP=printf \(aqfilter status=%s\(rsn\(aq \(dq$EXITCODE\(dq
.EE
.PP
The outer double quotes delimit the rc assignment word and are removed.
The single quotes remain inside that word and are later interpreted by
the trusted shell as part of the \f[CR]TRAP\f[R] command.
.SH LIMITS
Message limits are \f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_SIZE\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_HEADERS\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_BODY\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_LINE\f[R], and \f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_FIELD\f[R].
Their byte counts accept an optional binary \f[CR]K\f[R], \f[CR]M\f[R],
or \f[CR]G\f[R] suffix.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l r r.
T{
Variable
T}@T{
Default
T}@T{
Hard ceiling
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_SIZE\f[R]
T}@T{
64 MiB
T}@T{
256 MiB
T}
T{
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_HEADERS\f[R]
T}@T{
256 KiB
T}@T{
16 MiB
T}
T{
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_BODY\f[R]
T}@T{
64 MiB
T}@T{
256 MiB
T}
T{
\f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_LINE\f[R]
T}@T{
64 KiB
T}@T{
1 MiB
T}
T{
\f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_FIELD\f[R]
T}@T{
256 KiB
T}@T{
16 MiB
T}
.TE
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_SIZE\f[R]
Bounds the complete message in bytes: the original header bytes, the
header/body separator when present, and the body.
It is checked while stdin is read and again when a filter or native
header edit produces a replacement message.
A body may be streamed instead of retained, but its bytes still
contribute to this limit.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_HEADERS\f[R]
Bounds the complete raw header section in bytes, including physical line
endings and the empty line which separates headers from the body.
If EOF is reached before an empty separator line, every byte read is
treated as header data and contributes to this limit.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_MSG_BODY\f[R]
Bounds the bytes after the first empty header line.
The separator itself is not body data.
A message without a separator therefore has a zero\-byte body.
The limit is enforced for retained and streamed bodies and for
replacement messages returned by filters.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_LINE\f[R]
Bounds one physical header line, including its LF or CRLF ending.
A final unterminated line is also checked.
Folded continuations are separate physical lines for this limit.
Reading stops as soon as the next input chunk proves that the line would
be too large; EOF is not required.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_HEADER_FIELD\f[R]
Bounds one logical header field before folding is normalized for
matching.
The count is the sum of the original bytes in its first physical line
and every immediately following line beginning with space or tab,
including their line endings.
A continuation cannot evade the physical\-line limit: both limits apply.
.PP
Structural limits are \f[CR]LIMIT_MAX_ASSIGNMENTS\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_STATEMENTS\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_RC_RECIPES\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_CONDITIONS\f[R], \f[CR]LIMIT_RC_REGEXES\f[R],
\f[CR]LIMIT_RECIPE_CONDITIONS\f[R], and \f[CR]LIMIT_RECIPE_NESTING\f[R].
They accept decimal counts and cannot exceed their compiled ceilings.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l r r.
T{
Variable
T}@T{
Default
T}@T{
