.TE
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_MAX_ASSIGNMENTS\f[R]
Bounds assignments across the root rc file and every runtime
\f[CR]INCLUDERC\f[R] or \f[CR]SWITCHRC\f[R] file.
Ordinary assignment statements count.
A \f[CR]NAME=| command\f[R] recipe action also counts because it writes
command output to a variable.
The assignment which changes this limit is charged against the preceding
value, then its new value applies to following source text.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_STATEMENTS\f[R]
Bounds top\-level and nested assignment and recipe statements across all
rc files loaded for one message.
Blank lines, comments, condition lines, action lines, and header\-edit
commands are not separate statements.
An assignment that is also counted by \f[CR]LIMIT_MAX_ASSIGNMENTS\f[R]
consumes one statement; \f[CR]NAME=| command\f[R] consumes an assignment
but not an additional statement beyond its containing recipe.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_RECIPES\f[R]
Bounds recipe headers beginning with \f[CR]:0\f[R] across all loaded rc
files.
A recipe whose action is a block counts once, and every recipe inside
that block counts separately.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_CONDITIONS\f[R]
Bounds all recipe condition lines beginning with \f[CR]*\f[R] across all
loaded rc files.
Regex, size, program, and shell\-expanded \f[CR]$\f[R] conditions each
consume one entry.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_REGEXES\f[R]
Bounds conditions which can compile a regular expression.
An ordinary regex condition counts once.
A shell\-expanded \f[CR]$\f[R] condition also reserves one entry because
its expanded text is reparsed at runtime and may become a regex.
Size and program conditions do not count.
This limits the number of regex programs, not the number of capture
groups; those have separate fixed ceilings.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_RECIPE_CONDITIONS\f[R]
Bounds condition lines in one recipe.
The count starts again for each nested or following recipe.
The same condition is also charged to the file\-wide
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_CONDITIONS\f[R] count.
.TP
\f[CR]LIMIT_RECIPE_NESTING\f[R]
Bounds nested recipe\-action blocks.
A root\-level recipe is at depth zero; entering its \f[CR]{ ... }\f[R]
action creates depth one.
Plain recipe count is controlled separately by
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_RECIPES\f[R].
.IP
.EX
LIMIT_MSG_SIZE=32M
LIMIT_MSG_HEADERS=512K
LIMIT_HEADER_LINE=64K
LIMIT_RC_RECIPES=2048
LINEBUF=4096
.EE
.PP
All message and count limits accept zero.
A limit permits exactly that many bytes or items and rejects the next
one; values at \f[CR]limit \- 1\f[R] and \f[CR]limit\f[R] succeed when
no other limit is exceeded.
Structural limits and \f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R] take effect in statement order.
Lowering a structural limit below work already counted is accepted; the
next matching item is rejected.
Structural\-limit and \f[CR]LINEBUF\f[R] assignments are rejected inside
recipe blocks.
Runtime rc files share the counts accumulated by their caller and see
only settings which executed before they were loaded.
.PP
\f[CR]LIMIT_RC_SIZE\f[R] is reserved but not supported because an rc
file cannot safely choose the limit used to read itself.
Each rc file instead has a fixed 1 MiB ceiling, and all rc files loaded
while processing one message have a fixed combined 4 MiB ceiling.
See \f[B]Documentation/Limits.md\f[R] for the other fixed ceilings which
have no rc variable.
.PP
\f[CR]TIMEOUT\f[R], \f[CR]LOCKSLEEP\f[R], and \f[CR]LOCKTIMEOUT\f[R]
accept 1 through 86400 seconds; zero is rejected.
\f[CR]UMASK\f[R] accepts octal \f[CR]0000\f[R] through \f[CR]0777\f[R]
but can only remove permissions from restrictive backend modes.
.SH PRACTICAL EXAMPLES
The destination Maildirs used below, including their \f[CR]tmp\f[R],
\f[CR]new\f[R], and \f[CR]cur\f[R] subdirectories, must be created
before filtering.
.SS Mailing\-list delivery
Capture two components of \f[CR]List\-Id\f[R], reverse them in the
destination hierarchy, and retain a final inbox:
.IP
.EX
MAILDIR=/srv/mail

:0
* \(haList\-Id:.*<\(rs/([\(ha.>]+)\(rs.([\(ha.>]+)>
lists/$MATCH2/$MATCH1/

:0
inbox/
.EE
.SS Filtering and native header cleanup
Remove stale scanner results without \f[CR]formail\f[R], run a spam
filter over the complete message, then classify its generated header.
A failed waited filter sets status 75 and stops the current rc file
through \f[CR]HOST\f[R].
.IP
.EX
:0
headers {
    remove X\-Spam\-Flag
    remove X\-Spam\-Level
    remove X\-Spam\-Status
}

:0 fw
| bogofilter \-p \-u \-e \-c \(dq$HOME/.bogofilter.cf\(dq

:0 e
{
    EXITCODE=75
    HOST
}

:0
* \(haX\-Bogosity: (Spam|Yes)
spam/

:0
inbox/
.EE
.SS Bounded command\-selected folder
Backquoted destination fragments receive the complete message, have
trailing LF bytes removed, and are checked as part of the final path.
The selected year\-month Maildir must already exist.
.IP
.EX
MAILDIR=/srv/mail/archive

:0
maildir:\(gadate +%Y\-%m\(ga
.EE
.SS Header extraction for later rules
Extract a folded header natively when later rules need its unfolded
value.
This avoids starting a child and does not consume or stage the body.
.IP
.EX
:0
headers {
    extract unfolded To into MSG_TO
}

:0
* MSG_TO ?? user\(atexample\(rs.org
personal/
.EE
.SS Message\-selected rule file
An included file can depend on a capture made while filtering.
It is loaded only on the selected path, then processing returns after
its statements end.
.IP
.EX
RULE_ROOT=/srv/mail/rules

:0
* \(haX\-Account: \(rs/[A\-Za\-z0\-9_\-]+
{
    INCLUDERC=$RULE_ROOT/$MATCH.rc
}
.EE
.SH UNSUPPORTED FEATURES
There is no implicit \f[CR]DEFAULT\f[R] or \f[CR]ORGMAIL\f[R] delivery,
system mailbox, sendmail forward action, comsat notification, privileged
identity change, LMTP mode, MH folder, ordinary directory folder,
multi\-folder action, or weighted scoring.
.PP
When \f[B]\(enconfig\f[R] is omitted, \f[B]procmail\-rs\f[R] searches
the passwd home directory for \f[CR].config/procmail\-rs/config\f[R] and
then \f[CR].procmailrc\f[R].
This selects only the root rc file; it does not enable implicit message
delivery.
.PP
Reserved names for unsupported procmail behavior are rejected
explicitly.
Consult \f[CR]Documentation/Compatibility.md\f[R] for narrowed semantics
and known regular\-expression differences before using an existing
procmail rc file.
